#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Paints the three levels of Navigation's painted maps, for the world's zones and for the instances (dungeons and
raids) alike, by one pipeline (Doc/guides/map-style.md; Saneroki, 2026-10-21: strict maps of the layout, not free
concept art). Every subject has one frame, its box in the world, that all three levels share, so they lie over each
other exactly.

  close    the terrain, pixel-exact: the Atlas' own squares (dev/tools/atlas: one terrain tile at 2048 pixels, the
           game's minimap textures softened, its water flattened), the Atlas' words (atlas.toml) with the subject's
           colours and light, the Atlas' check against the game's water and lava (up to three seeds) and its
           colour pass. An instance's terrain is its own map's tiles with the indoor maps of the buildings they
           place laid over them, as the game's minimap draws them (dev/tools/indoor-art's parts, placed as Navigation
           places them). The samples paint the squares around the zone's main place, or over the whole instance.
  middle   a map in the manner of the game's own, laid exactly over the terrain, drawn in pencil on the
           interface's parchment, straight from above (levels.toml, [middle]): image 1 the terrain plan of the
           frame, image 2 the game's map art of it in the same frame (a zone's full map; an instance floor's map,
           its title banner painted over; where the game has none, another place's map for its manner only),
           image 3 the parchment. A zone in one picture, an instance floor by floor.
  far      a photorealistic aerial render, nearly straight down, its layout kept (levels.toml, [far]): image 1 the
           terrain plan, image 2 the middle level's drawing (an instance's floors laid where they lie), image 3 a
           piece of the close level's painting. A zone or an instance in one picture.

Each middle and far painting is checked twice and painted again with the next seed until one passes, TRIES at most:
  outlines  how well its edges agree with image 1's (their normalised correlation at OUTLINE pixels), against how
            well the game's own map agrees with the terrain (a middle level must reach MIDDLE of that, a far one FAR);
  looks     the vision model (qwen3vl-look) answers in JSON: writing in it; a pencil map on paper seen from above
            (middle); a real place, not a map or panels, seen from above, of the brief's kind (far).

Usage:
  dev/tools/map-layers inputs [--zones UIMAPS] [--instances MAPS] [--continents DIR --tables DIR --minimap DIR]
                              [--instance-files DIR] [--mapart DIR] [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers paint [--levels close,middle,far] [--only NAMES] [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers contact [--out DIR]
  dev/tools/map-layers status [--out DIR]

  inputs    the subjects' frames, terrain plans and the game's map art, remembered in the run's state.json (the
            game's folders default to the Atlas' run's); --zones 1436,1446 --instances 36,209,3065
  paint     the levels in order (the far level needs the other two), what is missing only; --only zone-1436,...
  contact   contact.jpg: a row per subject: image 1, the game's map, close, middle and far
  status    its first line: "<made> of <all> paintings made"

It writes, in the run's folder (default assets/generated/map-layers, never committed), per subject <name>
(zone-<uimap>, instance-<map> and its floors instance-<map>-<floor>):
  <name>/ground.png, art.png          the terrain plan and the game's map art in the frame (images 1 and 2)
  <name>/close/<i>-<j>.png, close.png the close squares, painted, and joined
  <name>/middle-<try>.png, far-<try>.png and checks.json (each try's outline agreement and the vision model's answer)
  state.json                          the paths and subjects
Needs Python with Pillow and NumPy, and ComfyUI; the game's files as dev/tools/atlas, dev/tools/indoor-art and
the private baseline extract them (an instance's terrain in a folder of its own: wdt/, adt/, adt-obj0/, minimap/,
wmo/, wmo-minimap/, tiles.jsonl).
"""
